\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


A link of three components in $\mathbb R^3$ is the disjoint union $L=C_1\cup C_2\cup C_3$ of three embedded closed curves. Each $C_i$ is unknotted if it is ambient isotopic to a round circle. A two-component unlink is ambient isotopic to two separated circles. The Borromean link type is the standard three-component link for which deleting any component leaves a two-component unlink, while all three components together are not an unlink; specifically the conventional Borromean link, rather than an arbitrary Brunnian link, is intended.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Do there exist three pairwise disjoint smooth unknotted closed curves $C_1,C_2,C_3\subset\mathbb R^3$, not all geometrically round circles, whose union has the Borromean link type? \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}\href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.09836v1}{[S2]}
The literal existence question has affirmative examples: the rectangular model in the cited source can have its corners smoothed while preserving its link type.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can three individually unknotted loops, with noncircular shapes permitted, form the standard Borromean link?

\subsection{Sources}
